course: dictionary
lesson: 15911
title: Unobjectionable
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Unobjectionable is a long word, but its parts help you. Un- means not, and objectionable means something that upsets or annoys people. So unobjectionable means not likely to upset or annoy anyone.
explain: Look at the clues in this sentence: «The film was unobjectionable, so the whole class could watch it.» The sentence tells you nobody would complain. It does not say the film was exciting, so it may have been a bit dull.
explain: This word is formal. You will meet it in reports, reviews and serious writing. In everyday talk, people would say fine, OK or harmless instead.
fact: Unobjectionable means not likely to upset or annoy anyone.
fact: It also means acceptable, though often not exciting.
fact: Un- means not, so unobjectionable is the opposite of objectionable.
fact: «An unobjectionable choice» is one nobody could complain about, even if nobody loves it.
fact: «Perfectly unobjectionable» means completely acceptable, with nothing to complain about.
exercise: «The film was unobjectionable, so the whole class could watch it.» What does unobjectionable mean? | *Not likely to upset anyone | Hard to understand | Loud and full of shouting | Very frightening
exercise: Which everyday word could take the place of unobjectionable in «an unobjectionable choice»? | Hidden | *Fine | Thrilling | Angry
exercise: Which part of unobjectionable means not? | -able | -ection | obj- | *un-
test: «The report used unobjectionable language, with no rude or upsetting words.» What does unobjectionable language mean? | Language that is very rude | Language in a foreign tongue | *Language nobody could complain about | Language full of slang
test: Why might a reviewer call a book unobjectionable but not exciting? | It is shocking and upsetting | It makes everyone cry | *It is acceptable, but nobody finds it thrilling | It is too long to read
exam: Which sentence uses unobjectionable correctly? | «The soup was unobjectionable: it made everyone shout.» | «The soup was unobjectionable: it was far too spicy to eat.» | «The soup was unobjectionable: it was covered in mud.» | *«The soup was unobjectionable: nobody disliked it, but nobody asked for more.»
